[center][size=6][b]Loadouts[/b][/size]
[size=3]Save your gear as loadouts and swap between them in one press, enchantments included.[/size][/center]

[line]

[size=5][b]Features[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Right on the inventory page[/b] - a Loadouts button sits beside Effects & Stats. It opens your loadouts over the item panel, built from the game's own panels, fonts and item frames, so it looks like part of the game.
[*][b]Save your gear[/b] - weapons, armor, artifacts and talismans, and each item's enchantment.
[*][b]Swap in one press[/b] - equip a loadout and its gear goes back on, in the slots it was saved in.
[*][b]Enchantments move with your gear[/b] - gear coming off is disenchanted, which gives its enchantment points back, and gear going on is enchanted again and upgraded to the tier it had. Spend your enchantment points on the gear you're actually wearing.
[*][b]Only what's different[/b] - gear that stays on isn't touched, and enchantments that are already right aren't redone. Each loadout shows what switching to it would change before you press anything.
[*][b]See each loadout at a glance[/b] - its gear in the game's rarity frames (enchanted gear marked) and its enchantments with their tiers.
[*][b]Rename, clear, save over[/b] - saving over a loadout or clearing one asks twice.
[*][b]Per character[/b] - each character has their own loadouts.
[*][b]Controller friendly[/b] - everything works with a controller, with the game's own button icons.
[/list]

[size=5][b]Controls[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Open the loadouts:[/b] on the inventory page, right stick click (controller), L (keyboard), or click the Loadouts button.
[*][b]In the loadouts:[/b] move with the stick or D-pad. A equips, X saves the gear you have on, Y renames, View clears, B closes. On the keyboard: Enter, S, R, Delete and Esc, or click.
[*][b]Next loadout:[/b] an optional key (unbound by default) that swaps to the next saved loadout anywhere.
[/list]

All keys can be changed in the game's Settings, Mods tab.

[size=5][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][url=https://www.nexusmods.com/minecraftdungeons2/mods/2]Blueprint Loader[/url] (2.0 or newer, for the settings page)
[/list]

[size=5][b]Install[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*]Install Blueprint Loader.
[*]Extract the [b]Loadouts[/b] folder from the zip into the game's mods folder, so that you have [i]~mods\Loadouts\Loadouts_P.pak[/i]:
Steam: [i]...\steamapps\common\Minecraft Dungeons II\Dungeons\Content\Paks\~mods[/i]
Xbox app: [i]...\Minecraft Dungeons II\Content\Dungeons\Content\Paks\~mods[/i]
[*]Start the game.
[/list]

To uninstall, delete the Loadouts folder. Your gear and enchantments stay as they are.

[size=5][b]Settings[/b][/size] (Settings, Mods tab, Loadouts)

[list]
[*]The key that opens the loadouts on the inventory page, and the "next loadout" key
[*]Number of loadouts: 2 to 6
[*]Move enchantments with the gear (on by default). Off: swapping only changes gear, and enchantments stay where they are.
[*]Update notice (on by default): tells you when a new version is out. It asks GitHub for the latest release when the game starts, at most every 6 hours; nothing else is sent.
[/list]

[size=5][b]Good to know[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Re-enchanting costs Spring Stones.[/b] Enchanting through a loadout costs what it costs at the Enchantsmith: about one Spring Stone per tier (a tier III enchantment takes 3). Disenchanting gives the enchantment points back, but not the Spring Stones. After each swap the loadouts show what it cost. Turn "move enchantments" off if you'd rather not spend any.
[*]Only gear that's in a saved loadout is ever disenchanted, so the mod can always put the enchantment back.
[*]Upgrade an enchantment while wearing a loadout and the loadout remembers the new tier when you swap away.
[*]If an item from a loadout is gone (salvaged or sold), the rest still goes on, and the loadout tells you what's missing.
[*]Renaming with a controller needs a keyboard, or Steam's on-screen keyboard.
[*]Swapping works in single player and in games you host. When you've joined someone else's game, the host's game decides what you can equip and enchant, so the loadouts tell you instead of swapping (saving and renaming still work).
[*]Made for game version 1.1.2. A game update may need an updated version of the mod.
[/list]

